cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "nextbrief: an evidence-gated daily brief across a portfolio of projects"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Hancheng
    family-names: Wang
    email: wanghancheng.ai@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-3432-5440"
repository-code: "https://github.com/hancheng-ai/nextbrief"
url: "https://github.com/hancheng-ai/nextbrief"
abstract: >-
  nextbrief produces a daily status brief across many concurrent projects from
  the files those projects already keep. It runs in three stages, and only the
  middle one uses a language model: deterministic sensing writes a snapshot of
  filesystem and git facts, a model interprets that snapshot into claims, and a
  deterministic renderer resolves every claim's evidence reference against the
  snapshot before printing it. Claims that cannot be resolved are dropped and
  logged rather than rendered, which moves "do not report progress that did not
  happen" from an instruction a model may drift from to a property of the
  pipeline. Three further gates flag proposals that contradict a project's
  declared non-goals, revert agent edits to human-only fields by diffing against
  git HEAD, and cap output length so the brief cannot grow past what a person
  will read.
keywords:
  - project management
  - large language models
  - hallucination mitigation
  - evidence grounding
  - agent containment
  - developer tools
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0rc4
date-released: "2026-07-27"
